1
00:01:06,430 --> 00:01:08,890
This is Mr Mathison.
He's a doctor.

2
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:14,650
- Are you okay?
- An ambulance is on the way.

3
00:01:14,730 --> 00:01:16,690
Thank you.

4
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:24,910
Is there a name yet?

5
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:28,160
Elijah.

6
00:01:32,620 --> 00:01:35,710
Is he supposed
to be crying like this?

7
00:01:35,830 --> 00:01:37,790
May I?

8
00:01:53,270 --> 00:01:55,230
Can I have him back?

9
00:01:59,860 --> 00:02:02,150
What happened during the delivery?

10
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:04,900
Nothing.
It was very quick.

11
00:02:04,990 --> 00:02:07,120
The babyjust wanted
to come right out.

12
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:09,200
And there were no problems.

13
00:02:11,410 --> 00:02:13,710
Did you drop him?

14
00:02:13,790 --> 00:02:15,750
What?

15
00:02:15,870 --> 00:02:18,000
Did you drop this baby?

16
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:20,040
Jesus Christ, no.

17
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:28,260
Inform the ambulance
that we have a situation.

18
00:02:28,340 --> 00:02:31,310
All right.
Molly.

19
00:02:31,390 --> 00:02:34,230
I've never seen this.

20
00:02:37,190 --> 00:02:41,020
It appears that your baby
has sustained some fractures...

21
00:02:41,150 --> 00:02:43,110
while inside your uterus.

22
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:48,660
His arms and his legs are broken.

23
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:40,960
This is Eastrail 177.

24
00:03:41,080 --> 00:03:43,210
Nextstop, Philadelphia.

25
00:04:17,160 --> 00:04:19,120
Are you alone?

26
00:04:20,830 --> 00:04:22,880
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

27
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:11,170
Someone left this magazine.

28
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:13,220
Want to take a look at it?

29
00:05:13,340 --> 00:05:15,300
Could I take a look at that one?

30
00:05:21,730 --> 00:05:23,600
Thanks.

31
00:05:23,690 --> 00:05:26,610
- You like sports?
- It's my field.

32
00:05:26,690 --> 00:05:28,650
I represent athletes.
I'm an agent.

33
00:05:30,690 --> 00:05:33,950
Are you looking for any
male synchronized swimmers?

34
00:05:34,070 --> 00:05:36,870
- I'm thinking about getting into that.
- Is that right?

35
00:05:40,290 --> 00:05:42,250
I'm afraid ofwater.

36
00:05:42,370 --> 00:05:44,330
Think that's a problem?

37
00:05:51,880 --> 00:05:54,800
You represent someone
in Philadelphia?

38
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:57,100
I'm meeting this player
from Temple University.

39
00:05:57,220 --> 00:05:59,180
He's a cornerback.

40
00:05:59,310 --> 00:06:03,060
This kid is 6'1 ", 210 pounds.

41
00:06:03,140 --> 00:06:07,310
He runs a 40 in 4,3 seconds.
He's gonna be a god.

42
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:10,400
Do you like football?

43
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:12,440
Not really.

44
00:06:23,750 --> 00:06:27,420
- I'm David Dunn.
- Kelly.

45
00:06:27,540 --> 00:06:30,290
- Hi.
- Hi, nice to meet you.

46
00:06:30,380 --> 00:06:32,340
Nice - Nice to meet you.

47
00:06:39,970 --> 00:06:42,520
How long are you gonna be
in Philadelphia?

48
00:06:50,690 --> 00:06:53,780
- I'm married.
- Great.

49
00:06:53,900 --> 00:06:56,740
- I'm sorry.
- What are you talking about?

50
00:06:59,530 --> 00:07:01,740
I--

51
00:07:01,870 --> 00:07:06,160
I think you misunderstood
what I was, uh...

52
00:07:06,250 --> 00:07:08,370
- s-saying--
- I know.

53
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,300
I think I'm gonna find another seat.

54
00:07:13,420 --> 00:07:15,880
I-I didn't mean, uh--

55
00:08:55,520 --> 00:08:58,780
That's what he failed to realize.
I look classy, not trashy.

56
00:08:58,900 --> 00:09:03,700
I look classy too. What about me?
Don't I look classy?

57
00:09:03,820 --> 00:09:06,200
But I'm afraid you forgot this!

58
00:09:06,330 --> 00:09:10,500
- And this! And--
- Where has you been, Banana?

59
00:09:10,580 --> 00:09:12,790
Now I've got you, weasel.

60
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:17,630
Some cars are still on fire,
and the debris is everywhere.

61
00:09:17,710 --> 00:09:19,590
We are notsure at this time, uh...

62
00:09:19,710 --> 00:09:22,180
how rescue personnel will be able
to get on scene at all.

63
00:09:22,300 --> 00:09:24,840
If you're just tuning in,
Eastrail train number 177...

64
00:09:24,970 --> 00:09:27,350
has derailed
just outside Philadelphia.

65
00:09:27,470 --> 00:09:29,770
And we're bringing you
live coverage from Skycam 4.

66
00:09:29,890 --> 00:09:32,190
Yes, that is-- It appears
to be a rescue ambulance...

67
00:09:32,310 --> 00:09:35,230
and there are more behind it
I can see on the access road.

68
00:09:35,310 --> 00:09:37,770
I don't know how close they're
gonna be able toget, David.

69
00:09:37,900 --> 00:09:40,690
These are woodsand, uh, trees.

70
00:09:40,780 --> 00:09:43,150
It's gonna be a very
difficult rescue effort.

71
00:09:59,300 --> 00:10:01,260
Try to hack some of that off.

72
00:10:14,190 --> 00:10:17,860
Hi. I'm Dr Dubin.

73
00:10:17,980 --> 00:10:21,650
You're in the emergency room in
the Philadelphia City Hospital.

74
00:10:21,780 --> 00:10:23,740
You were in a serious accident.

75
00:10:24,900 --> 00:10:27,200
Look at me.

76
00:10:27,280 --> 00:10:29,240
How are you feeling?

77
00:10:31,160 --> 00:10:33,370
- Okay.
- Good.

78
00:10:33,450 --> 00:10:35,920
I'm gonna ask you some questions.

79
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:39,750
Have you ever had any heart
or asthma problems in the past?

80
00:10:39,880 --> 00:10:44,050
- No.
- Kidney or renal problems?

81
00:10:44,130 --> 00:10:46,590
- No.
- Any allergies?

82
00:10:49,010 --> 00:10:50,970
No.

83
00:10:55,810 --> 00:10:57,770
Where were you sitting
on the train?

84
00:11:01,820 --> 00:11:04,650
- Against the window.
- In the passenger car?

85
00:11:06,900 --> 00:11:08,870
Yeah.

86
00:11:11,120 --> 00:11:14,870
- Where are the other passengers?
- Was your family travelling with you?

87
00:11:17,540 --> 00:11:19,420
No.

88
00:11:19,540 --> 00:11:22,250
Did you get up from your seat?

89
00:11:27,880 --> 00:11:30,890
Are you certain
you were in the passenger car?

90
00:11:36,180 --> 00:11:38,140
Yes.

91
00:11:45,740 --> 00:11:48,570
Why are you looking at me like that?

92
00:11:48,650 --> 00:11:50,700
Your train derailed.

93
00:11:50,780 --> 00:11:53,530
Some kind of malfunction.

94
00:11:53,620 --> 00:11:56,580
They only found
two people alive so far--

95
00:11:56,660 --> 00:11:58,620
you and this man.

96
00:12:01,040 --> 00:12:04,380
His skull was cracked open and
most of his left side was crushed.

97
00:12:06,550 --> 00:12:08,670
And to answer your question,
there are two reasons...

98
00:12:08,760 --> 00:12:11,760
why I'm looking at you like this.

99
00:12:11,890 --> 00:12:14,680
One, because it seems
in a few minutes...

100
00:12:14,760 --> 00:12:19,060
that you will officially be
the only survivor of this train wreck.

101
00:12:19,140 --> 00:12:22,060
And two...

102
00:12:22,150 --> 00:12:24,110
because you didn't break one bone.

103
00:12:25,570 --> 00:12:27,530
You don't have a scratch on you.

104
00:14:13,630 --> 00:14:15,590
David?

105
00:14:18,260 --> 00:14:22,180
What happened in New York?

106
00:14:24,020 --> 00:14:26,400
I don't think I got the job.

107
00:14:28,230 --> 00:14:31,400
I'm still going to, uh--

108
00:14:31,480 --> 00:14:34,240
I'm still moving there.

109
00:14:34,320 --> 00:14:36,280
Just--

110
00:14:37,450 --> 00:14:39,410
Uh, just - just not now.

111
00:14:44,460 --> 00:14:46,580
Good night.

112
00:14:49,750 --> 00:14:51,710
Good night.

113
00:15:03,730 --> 00:15:05,770
Sara Elaston...

114
00:15:05,850 --> 00:15:09,020
social worker at
Broad and Locust Community Centre.

115
00:15:10,270 --> 00:15:12,400
We pray foryour soul.

116
00:15:14,610 --> 00:15:17,700
Kevin Elliot, businessman...

117
00:15:17,820 --> 00:15:20,700
father of six.

118
00:15:20,780 --> 00:15:22,740
We pray for your soul.

119
00:15:24,370 --> 00:15:26,500
Glen Stevens...

120
00:15:26,620 --> 00:15:29,880
researcher in the area
of leukemia...

121
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:31,960
at Drexel University.

122
00:15:33,800 --> 00:15:35,760
We pray for your soul.

123
00:15:37,590 --> 00:15:39,720
Jennifer Pennyman...

124
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:44,020
third-grade teacher
at Jefferson Elementary.

125
00:15:46,230 --> 00:15:48,190
We pray for your soul.

126
00:16:55,290 --> 00:16:58,460
- Yes?
- Is Noel in, ma'am?

127
00:16:58,550 --> 00:17:00,510
No, sir, he is not.

128
00:17:01,970 --> 00:17:04,800
I read about you in the paper.

129
00:17:04,890 --> 00:17:08,140
- What?
- I was in an accident once too.

130
00:17:08,220 --> 00:17:11,230
A horse almost trampled me to death.

131
00:17:11,310 --> 00:17:13,270
- Wow.
- Had him put down.

132
00:17:15,980 --> 00:17:17,940
That's a sad story.

133
00:17:20,860 --> 00:17:25,740
Could-- Could you ask Noel
something for me, please?

134
00:17:25,870 --> 00:17:27,990
Proceed.

135
00:17:28,120 --> 00:17:30,160
Would you ask him to check
how many sick days I've taken...

136
00:17:30,250 --> 00:17:32,370
since I've worked here?

137
00:17:32,460 --> 00:17:35,330
- That the entire message?
- Yes, ma'am.

138
00:17:39,260 --> 00:17:41,220
Thankyou.

139
00:18:19,500 --> 00:18:21,300
Hey, Noel.

140
00:18:21,380 --> 00:18:23,590
What, you hit your head on that train?

141
00:18:23,720 --> 00:18:25,680
Get your brain
to start working again?

142
00:18:27,260 --> 00:18:29,970
- What?
- Forty dollars.

143
00:18:31,600 --> 00:18:34,310
- Forty dollars what?
- You're gettin' a $40-a-week raise.

144
00:18:34,390 --> 00:18:36,350
That's it.

145
00:18:37,520 --> 00:18:40,690
I checked. You were right.
You've never taken a sick day.

146
00:18:40,820 --> 00:18:44,490
Five years, no sick day.
I get it. You want a raise.

147
00:18:44,610 --> 00:18:46,570
Smart way to make your point.

148
00:19:13,310 --> 00:19:15,850
- Is Joseph okay?
- Yeah.

149
00:19:15,980 --> 00:19:18,520
- He's asleep.
- Oh.

150
00:19:22,530 --> 00:19:25,610
I wanted to ask you a question.

151
00:19:25,740 --> 00:19:27,950
It's gonna sound a little strange,
but, uh...

152
00:19:28,030 --> 00:19:30,990
just think about it
for a second, okay?

153
00:19:31,080 --> 00:19:33,790
Okay.

154
00:19:33,870 --> 00:19:36,000
When's the last time I was sick?

155
00:19:36,120 --> 00:19:38,080
Do you remember?

156
00:19:41,670 --> 00:19:43,630
Um, I-I don't know.

157
00:19:45,720 --> 00:19:48,550
It's been a while.

158
00:19:48,630 --> 00:19:52,390
I haven't been sick this year,
I know that.

159
00:19:52,510 --> 00:19:54,640
Okay.

160
00:19:54,720 --> 00:19:56,680
Do you remember me gettin' sick?

161
00:19:59,900 --> 00:20:02,190
Um...

162
00:20:02,270 --> 00:20:04,570
not a specific day.

163
00:20:04,650 --> 00:20:06,860
And what's-what's this about?

164
00:20:09,160 --> 00:20:12,660
Audrey, do you remember me
ever getting sick?

165
00:20:12,780 --> 00:20:15,660
In the three years
we lived in this house?

166
00:20:15,750 --> 00:20:17,790
In the old apartment?

167
00:20:17,910 --> 00:20:20,880
Before Joseph was born?
Before we ever got married?

168
00:20:25,210 --> 00:20:27,170
I-I can't remember.

169
00:20:29,090 --> 00:20:31,390
Don't you think
that's kind of weird...

170
00:20:31,510 --> 00:20:35,680
not remembering one cold
or a-a fever or a sore throat?

171
00:20:37,980 --> 00:20:39,940
What do you think it means?

172
00:20:44,730 --> 00:20:47,650
Um...

173
00:20:47,740 --> 00:20:51,570
I think it means
I'm probably too tired to remember.

174
00:21:33,030 --> 00:21:37,410
No more sittin' in this room.
I let it go on long enough.

175
00:21:37,490 --> 00:21:40,660
I'm not goin' out there any more.
I'm not getting hurt again.

176
00:21:40,750 --> 00:21:45,040
- This was the last time, I told you.
- You can't do anything about that.

177
00:21:45,170 --> 00:21:47,460
You might fall between
this chair and that television.

178
00:21:47,590 --> 00:21:50,590
If that's what God has planned for you,
that's what's gonna happen.

179
00:21:50,670 --> 00:21:53,050
You can't hide from it
sittin' in a room.

180
00:21:57,140 --> 00:22:00,980
They call me Mr Glass at school
because I break like glass.

181
00:22:06,110 --> 00:22:08,070
You make this decision now...

182
00:22:09,530 --> 00:22:11,490
to be afraid...

183
00:22:13,150 --> 00:22:15,280
and you will never turn back.

184
00:22:17,030 --> 00:22:21,830
Your whole life,
you will always be afraid.

185
00:22:36,680 --> 00:22:39,220
- I got a present for you.
- Why?

186
00:22:40,930 --> 00:22:42,890
Forget why.

187
00:22:45,520 --> 00:22:47,480
You want it or not?

188
00:22:50,480 --> 00:22:52,690
Well, go get it, then.

189
00:22:52,780 --> 00:22:55,740
Where is it?

190
00:22:55,820 --> 00:22:58,450
On a bench...

191
00:22:58,530 --> 00:23:00,490
across the street.

192
00:23:14,590 --> 00:23:16,550
Someone's gonna take it.

193
00:23:18,590 --> 00:23:21,350
Then you better get out there soon.

194
00:24:35,800 --> 00:24:37,760
I bought a whole bunch.

195
00:24:42,300 --> 00:24:44,350
There'll be one of these
waitin' for you...

196
00:24:44,470 --> 00:24:46,600
every time you want
to come out here.

197
00:24:53,360 --> 00:24:56,440
They say this one
has a surprise ending.

198
00:25:05,370 --> 00:25:08,370
This is from Fritz Campion's
own library.

199
00:25:08,500 --> 00:25:13,380
This is before the first issue of
the comic book hit the stands in 1968.

200
00:25:13,500 --> 00:25:17,550
It's a classic depiction
of good versus evil.

201
00:25:17,630 --> 00:25:21,630
Notice the square jaw of Slayer,
common in most comic heroes.

202
00:25:21,720 --> 00:25:25,470
And the slightly disproportionate size
of Jaguaro's head to his body.

203
00:25:25,560 --> 00:25:29,640
This again is common,
but only in villains.

204
00:25:29,730 --> 00:25:32,100
The thing to notice about this piece...

205
00:25:32,230 --> 00:25:36,230
the thing that makes it
very, very special...

206
00:25:36,320 --> 00:25:39,400
is its realistic depiction
of its figures.

207
00:25:39,490 --> 00:25:41,530
When the characters
reached the magazine...

208
00:25:41,610 --> 00:25:43,660
they were exaggerated...

209
00:25:43,780 --> 00:25:45,990
as always happens.

210
00:25:46,120 --> 00:25:49,870
This is vintage.

211
00:25:52,750 --> 00:25:54,710
Well...

212
00:25:59,840 --> 00:26:02,300
wrap it up.

213
00:26:02,430 --> 00:26:04,800
You've made a considerably
wise decision.

214
00:26:10,270 --> 00:26:12,230
Well, my kid's gonna go berserk.

215
00:26:15,810 --> 00:26:17,860
Once again, please?

216
00:26:17,940 --> 00:26:20,740
My son Jeb--
it's a gift for him.

217
00:26:20,820 --> 00:26:23,700
- How old is Jeb?
- He's four.

218
00:26:26,700 --> 00:26:28,740
No.

219
00:26:28,870 --> 00:26:32,620
No, no, no, no, no.

220
00:26:34,120 --> 00:26:36,500
You need to go. Now.

221
00:26:38,290 --> 00:26:40,420
What'd - What'd I say?

222
00:26:40,510 --> 00:26:43,840
Do you see any Teletubbies in here?

223
00:26:43,930 --> 00:26:45,970
Do you see a slender plastic tag
clipped to my shirt...

224
00:26:46,050 --> 00:26:48,180
with my name printed on it?

225
00:26:48,300 --> 00:26:51,220
Did you see a little Asian child with
a blank expression sitting outside...

226
00:26:51,310 --> 00:26:55,060
in a mechanical helicopter that shakes
when you put quarters in it?

227
00:26:55,140 --> 00:26:58,730
No? Well, that's what
you see at a toy store...

228
00:26:58,860 --> 00:27:00,820
and you must think
you're in a toy store because...

229
00:27:00,940 --> 00:27:04,450
you're here shopping
for an infant named Jeb.

230
00:27:04,570 --> 00:27:07,570
Now, one of us
has made a gross error...

231
00:27:07,660 --> 00:27:10,830
and wasted the other person's
valuable time.

232
00:27:10,950 --> 00:27:13,080
This is an art gallery,
my friend...

233
00:27:13,160 --> 00:27:16,920
and this is a piece ofart.

234
00:27:32,270 --> 00:27:34,230
We're by appointment only.

235
00:27:36,020 --> 00:27:38,650
- I got a card from this store.
- Congratulations.

236
00:27:38,730 --> 00:27:42,320
You have a mailbox.
The exhibition isn't for two weeks.

237
00:27:42,440 --> 00:27:45,280
This one was under
the windshield wiper of my car.

238
00:27:48,160 --> 00:27:51,240
How certain are you that
you've never taken ill?

239
00:27:51,330 --> 00:27:53,870
Seventy-five percent.

240
00:27:53,950 --> 00:27:57,370
Well, that's not
certain at all, is it?

241
00:27:58,830 --> 00:28:01,750
That's hole number one.

242
00:28:01,840 --> 00:28:05,010
I'm going to be extremely skeptical
about all this.

243
00:28:05,130 --> 00:28:07,090
About all what?

244
00:28:08,630 --> 00:28:11,800
I'm assuming you've never been injured.
Would I be wrong in that assumption?

245
00:28:11,930 --> 00:28:13,890
Dad's been injured.

246
00:28:15,220 --> 00:28:18,230
Is the child correct?

247
00:28:18,310 --> 00:28:22,440
Yes, sir. In college,
I was in a car accident.

248
00:28:22,520 --> 00:28:27,400
- Was it serious?
- He couldn't play football any more.

249
00:28:27,490 --> 00:28:29,450
That's hole number two.

250
00:28:31,280 --> 00:28:33,240
That's a big one.

251
00:28:36,870 --> 00:28:40,790
Mr Price, can we talk about
the note that you left on my car?

252
00:28:40,920 --> 00:28:44,170
I've studied the form
of comics intimately.

253
00:28:44,250 --> 00:28:46,300
I've spent a third of my life
in a hospital bed...

254
00:28:46,380 --> 00:28:49,550
with nothing else to do but read.

255
00:28:49,630 --> 00:28:51,590
I believe comics
are our last link...

256
00:28:51,720 --> 00:28:55,390
to an ancient way
of passing on history.

257
00:28:55,520 --> 00:28:57,640
The Egyptians drew on walls.

258
00:28:57,770 --> 00:29:02,020
Countries all over the world still pass
on knowledge through pictorial forms.

259
00:29:02,110 --> 00:29:04,230
I believe comics
are a form of history...

260
00:29:04,360 --> 00:29:07,940
that someone somewhere
felt or experienced.

261
00:29:08,030 --> 00:29:10,320
Then, of course,
those experiences and that history...

262
00:29:10,450 --> 00:29:13,450
got chewed up in the commercial
machine, got jazzed up...

263
00:29:13,570 --> 00:29:16,450
made titillating,
cartooned for the sale rack.

264
00:29:17,830 --> 00:29:21,250
This city has seen its share
of disasters.

265
00:29:21,330 --> 00:29:23,790
I watched the aftermath
of that plane crash.

266
00:29:23,920 --> 00:29:26,460
I watched the carnage
of the hotel fiire.

267
00:29:26,550 --> 00:29:31,430
I watched the news waiting to hear
a very specifiic combination of words...

268
00:29:31,550 --> 00:29:34,010
but they never came.

269
00:29:34,140 --> 00:29:37,890
Then one day I saw a news story
about a train accident...

270
00:29:37,970 --> 00:29:40,020
and I heard them.

271
00:29:40,100 --> 00:29:42,150
"There is a sole survivor...

272
00:29:42,270 --> 00:29:45,360
and he is miraculously unharmed."

273
00:29:48,400 --> 00:29:51,820
I have something called
osteogenesis imperfecta.

274
00:29:51,950 --> 00:29:53,910
It's a genetic disorder.

275
00:29:54,030 --> 00:29:55,990
I don't make a particular
protein very well...

276
00:29:56,120 --> 00:29:58,660
and it makes my bones
very low in density.

277
00:29:58,750 --> 00:30:01,040
Very easy to break.

278
00:30:01,120 --> 00:30:03,670
I've had 54 breaks in my life...

279
00:30:03,750 --> 00:30:08,550
and I have the tamest version
of this disorder, type one.

280
00:30:08,630 --> 00:30:12,300
There are type two,
type three, type four.

281
00:30:12,430 --> 00:30:14,390
Type fours don't last very long.

282
00:30:16,050 --> 00:30:19,060
So that's how it popped into my head.

283
00:30:19,140 --> 00:30:21,980
If there is someone
like me in the world...

284
00:30:22,060 --> 00:30:24,270
and I'm at one end
of the spectrum...

285
00:30:24,400 --> 00:30:26,440
couldn't there be someone else...

286
00:30:26,520 --> 00:30:28,730
the opposite of me
at the other end?

287
00:30:28,860 --> 00:30:33,240
Someone who doesn't get sick, who
doesn't get hurt like the rest of us?

288
00:30:33,320 --> 00:30:37,080
And he probably
doesn't even know it.

289
00:30:37,200 --> 00:30:42,410
The kind of person
these stories are about.

290
00:30:42,500 --> 00:30:47,630
A person put here
to protect the rest of us.

291
00:30:47,710 --> 00:30:49,750
To guard us.

292
00:30:52,220 --> 00:30:56,760
- You think my dad's a real--
- I don't think anything right now.

293
00:30:56,890 --> 00:30:59,350
It's a possibility--

294
00:30:59,430 --> 00:31:01,390
one with many holes.

295
00:31:04,980 --> 00:31:07,650
Joseph, don't take
another sip of that water.

296
00:31:10,820 --> 00:31:12,860
- Go throw it in the trash.
- But Dad--

297
00:31:12,990 --> 00:31:14,950
Do it now, please.

298
00:31:24,620 --> 00:31:28,210
I see guys like you
all the time in my work.

299
00:31:28,330 --> 00:31:31,840
You find someone you want
to take advantage of...

300
00:31:31,920 --> 00:31:35,340
you tell them a fantastic story...

301
00:31:35,470 --> 00:31:38,340
and somewhere in there you say it:

302
00:31:38,430 --> 00:31:41,390
"I just need
your credit card number...

303
00:31:41,470 --> 00:31:43,430
a small down payment."

304
00:31:46,140 --> 00:31:48,100
This morning was the first morning
that I could remember...

305
00:31:48,230 --> 00:31:50,190
that I didn't open my eyes
and feel...

306
00:31:53,070 --> 00:31:55,190
sadness.

307
00:31:58,780 --> 00:32:01,950
I thought the person that wrote
that note had an answer for me.

308
00:32:08,880 --> 00:32:11,880
I'm gonna leave now.

309
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:13,960
Good luck with your exhibition.

310
00:32:14,090 --> 00:32:16,550
So, what kind ofjob
do you have, David?

311
00:32:16,670 --> 00:32:18,720
You said you've met guys like me
in your work.

312
00:32:18,800 --> 00:32:23,350
- What exactly is it that you do?
- I work at the University Stadium.

313
00:32:23,470 --> 00:32:26,020
I'm a security guard.

314
00:34:41,740 --> 00:34:43,950
Hi.

315
00:34:44,030 --> 00:34:46,870
I've come to a decision.

316
00:34:46,950 --> 00:34:49,330
Okay.

317
00:34:49,410 --> 00:34:51,950
I just wanna ask you something, okay?

318
00:34:52,040 --> 00:34:55,540
And you can be totally honest.

319
00:34:55,670 --> 00:34:59,250
I'm prepared for any answer,
and-and it won't affect me.

320
00:35:01,880 --> 00:35:04,680
Have you been with anyone?

321
00:35:04,760 --> 00:35:06,970
I mean, since we've
been having problems.

322
00:35:07,100 --> 00:35:09,060
The answer won't affect me.

323
00:35:12,310 --> 00:35:14,980
I just need to know, you know?

324
00:35:15,060 --> 00:35:17,020
I mean, it won't affect me
either way.

325
00:35:19,480 --> 00:35:21,440
No.

326
00:35:29,370 --> 00:35:31,580
Sorry, David.
I'm sorry.

327
00:35:36,920 --> 00:35:39,040
Anyway...

328
00:35:39,170 --> 00:35:41,130
my decision is...

329
00:35:44,420 --> 00:35:46,380
that I want to start again.

330
00:35:50,050 --> 00:35:52,810
And I want it to be
like it was in the beginning.

331
00:35:56,190 --> 00:35:59,520
And it's a big deal that
you walked away from that train.

332
00:36:02,480 --> 00:36:04,530
I feel like it's a second chance.

333
00:36:10,740 --> 00:36:14,000
And if you feel like
asking me out sometime...

334
00:36:14,080 --> 00:36:16,040
that'd be okay.

335
00:36:18,540 --> 00:36:20,500
Okay.

336
00:36:33,220 --> 00:36:35,770
- Dunn.
- Go for Dunn.

337
00:36:35,850 --> 00:36:40,150
Yeah, it's Jenkins. Look, we got a guy
over at gate 17-C with a bogus ticket.

338
00:36:40,230 --> 00:36:44,280
Says he knows you.
He won't tell me his name.

339
00:36:44,360 --> 00:36:47,070
What's he look like?

340
00:36:47,150 --> 00:36:49,360
Why is it, do you think, that
of all the professions in the world...

341
00:36:49,450 --> 00:36:51,410
you chose protection?

342
00:36:53,530 --> 00:36:56,330
- You are a very strange man.
- You could have been a tax accountant.

343
00:36:56,410 --> 00:36:59,290
You could have owned your own gym. You
could've opened a chain of restaurants.

344
00:36:59,370 --> 00:37:01,750
You could've done
one of 10,000 things.

345
00:37:01,880 --> 00:37:06,010
But in the end,
you chose to protect people.

346
00:37:06,090 --> 00:37:08,550
You made that decision,
and I find that...

347
00:37:08,670 --> 00:37:10,640
very, very interesting.

348
00:37:11,970 --> 00:37:14,890
Now all I need is
your credit card number.

349
00:37:16,020 --> 00:37:17,980
That last part was a joke.

350
00:37:22,190 --> 00:37:26,650
- You're a big sports fan, are you?
- It's starting to intrigue me.

351
00:37:27,990 --> 00:37:30,360
It gets heaviest
ten minutes before kickoff.

352
00:37:42,790 --> 00:37:45,090
Programme here.
Programme.

353
00:37:45,170 --> 00:37:47,130
Programme.

354
00:37:48,970 --> 00:37:52,720
Programme. Programme here.

355
00:37:52,840 --> 00:37:54,800
- Rich, why don't we pat down?
- Right.

356
00:37:54,930 --> 00:37:56,890
-Just give me a minute.
- Is there a problem?

357
00:37:57,010 --> 00:37:58,980
I'm sorry.
I'm gonna have to search you.

358
00:37:59,100 --> 00:38:02,440
- It's nothing personal.
- The tall guy in the camouflage jacket.

359
00:38:02,560 --> 00:38:07,110
Sometimes people carry weapons in here,
and they drink too much.

360
00:38:07,190 --> 00:38:11,820
If the team's not doing good,
bad things can happen.

361
00:38:11,950 --> 00:38:15,200
We do pat-downs of the crowd
to discourage people from carrying.

362
00:38:16,620 --> 00:38:19,250
If he's carrying,
he'll step out of line.

363
00:38:32,380 --> 00:38:34,340
Yeah, step right up here.

364
00:38:43,520 --> 00:38:48,320
Okay. I got you a seat up
in the yellow section.

365
00:38:48,400 --> 00:38:53,200
It's in the nosebleeds,
but at least you won't get spit on.

366
00:38:53,320 --> 00:38:55,530
- All right.
- How'd you know that guy you bumped...

367
00:38:55,660 --> 00:38:57,620
was carrying a weapon?

368
00:38:59,790 --> 00:39:02,000
I don't know.

369
00:39:02,120 --> 00:39:06,500
Maybe it was that camouflage
armyjacket he was wearing.

370
00:39:06,630 --> 00:39:09,380
Those guys like to carry
hunting knives and stuff for show.

371
00:39:09,460 --> 00:39:12,800
You thought he was carrying a knife?

372
00:39:12,920 --> 00:39:14,261
I thought he was
carrying something, yeah.

373
00:39:14,394 --> 00:39:16,010
But not a knife.

374
00:39:19,640 --> 00:39:24,600
I got a picture of a silver gun
with a black grip...

375
00:39:24,690 --> 00:39:26,900
tucked in his pants.

376
00:39:27,020 --> 00:39:29,400
You know, like on TV.

377
00:39:30,860 --> 00:39:33,700
You have good instincts
for things like that?

378
00:39:33,780 --> 00:39:34,591
Like what?

379
00:39:34,826 --> 00:39:37,200
Knowing when people
have done something wrong.

380
00:39:40,700 --> 00:39:43,790
- Yeah.
- Have you ever tried to develop it?

381
00:39:45,790 --> 00:39:49,880
- I don't know what you're asking me.
- Your skill.

382
00:39:49,960 --> 00:39:52,090
Look, I gotta be down on
the sidelines during the game.

383
00:39:52,210 --> 00:39:54,420
- You can get to your seat right down--
- Characters in comics...

384
00:39:54,550 --> 00:39:57,010
are often attributed special powers--

385
00:39:57,140 --> 00:40:01,600
invisibility, X-ray vision,
things of that sort.

386
00:40:01,680 --> 00:40:04,230
Okay.

387
00:40:04,350 --> 00:40:08,980
- I don't wanna play this game any more.
- It's an exaggeration of the truth.

388
00:40:09,060 --> 00:40:14,280
Maybe it's based on something
as simple as instinct.

389
00:40:14,360 --> 00:40:16,990
- But he might not have been carrying anything.
- But he might have been carrying...

390
00:40:17,070 --> 00:40:19,950
a silver gun with a black grip
tucked in his pants.

391
00:40:20,030 --> 00:40:22,870
- Dunn.
- I gotta go.

392
00:40:25,290 --> 00:40:27,500
Yeah, I know.
I'll be right down.

393
00:40:27,620 --> 00:40:30,790
- One last question.
- What?

394
00:40:30,920 --> 00:40:33,300
That car accident you were in.

395
00:40:33,380 --> 00:40:35,340
Was there anyone else involved?

396
00:40:37,590 --> 00:40:40,930
Yeah.
My wife, Audrey.

397
00:40:41,010 --> 00:40:43,060
She was in the car with me.

398
00:40:45,180 --> 00:40:48,100
Have a good life, Elijah.

399
00:40:48,190 --> 00:40:51,520
Next time, try to buy your tickets
at an authorized sales location.

400
00:40:51,650 --> 00:40:55,690
- Dunn.
- I'm on my way.

401
00:40:59,740 --> 00:41:01,950
...into the investigation
of train 177...

402
00:41:02,080 --> 00:41:05,240
being conductedat the Eastrail
holding warehouses.

403
00:41:05,330 --> 00:41:07,870
The citycouncil has
stepped up plans to pass a bill...

404
00:41:07,960 --> 00:41:11,290
requiring higher standards
for inspection and maintenance...

405
00:41:11,380 --> 00:41:14,050
of all rail transportation
for Philadelphia...

406
00:41:14,130 --> 00:41:16,090
and its outlying districts.

407
00:41:23,890 --> 00:41:25,520
Hold up a second!

408
00:42:20,570 --> 00:42:23,200
I just want to ask you something.

409
00:43:32,930 --> 00:43:35,600
Dad! Hey, Dad!

410
00:43:42,530 --> 00:43:44,490
Dad!

411
00:43:55,580 --> 00:43:59,340
Joseph, do you know how mad your mother would
be if she knew you were playing football?

412
00:43:59,420 --> 00:44:02,260
Are you gonna tell?

413
00:44:02,340 --> 00:44:05,760
You wanna play last ofthe downs?
We got a big guy like you.

414
00:44:05,880 --> 00:44:10,010
You could play on opposite sides.
He's Potter's cousin.

415
00:44:10,100 --> 00:44:12,640
He's starting cornerback
at Temple University.

416
00:44:12,720 --> 00:44:14,770
He's going pro in the draft.

417
00:44:14,890 --> 00:44:18,310
They say he can run the
40 Yards in 4,3 seconds.

418
00:44:18,440 --> 00:44:20,310
Yeah, I've heard.

419
00:44:20,440 --> 00:44:22,400
You can beat him, Dad.

420
00:44:22,530 --> 00:44:24,990
- Let's beat him.
- I'm gonna go in.

421
00:44:25,070 --> 00:44:27,700
Just play one set ofdowns.
I told them you were great.

422
00:44:27,780 --> 00:44:29,740
- Why'd you do that?
-Just one.

423
00:44:29,870 --> 00:44:31,990
I'm gonna go in.
I've got some things to do.

424
00:44:32,080 --> 00:44:34,870
- What things?
- I'm gonna work out.

425
00:44:34,950 --> 00:44:36,580
- I'll help you.
- No, I-I don't need--

426
00:44:36,660 --> 00:44:40,420
I can't play, guys!
I'm working out with my dad!

427
00:44:40,540 --> 00:44:43,550
Go, go.
Here we go! Ready?

428
00:44:43,630 --> 00:44:46,800
Hut! You're doing good.
Right there! Catch!

429
00:44:46,920 --> 00:44:49,220
I've got to run

430
00:44:49,300 --> 00:44:51,850
To keep from hiding

431
00:44:51,970 --> 00:44:54,270
And I'm bound

432
00:44:54,390 --> 00:44:56,850
To keep on riding

433
00:44:56,980 --> 00:45:01,610
And I've got one more silverdollar

434
00:45:01,690 --> 00:45:03,820
How much did you put on there?

435
00:45:12,700 --> 00:45:16,450
You put too much.
That's 250 pounds.

436
00:45:16,580 --> 00:45:20,330
How much can you lift?

437
00:45:20,460 --> 00:45:22,500
That's the most I ever lifted.

438
00:45:25,000 --> 00:45:27,800
That could've been dangerous,Joseph.

439
00:45:27,880 --> 00:45:30,260
Why don't you go upstairs now
and let me finish up, okay?

440
00:45:30,340 --> 00:45:33,140
I'll take it off.
I'll help you right.

441
00:45:37,600 --> 00:45:41,270
You think you could have
beaten up Bruce Lee?

442
00:45:41,350 --> 00:45:43,150
No.

443
00:45:43,270 --> 00:45:45,730
I mean, if you knew karate.

444
00:45:45,860 --> 00:45:47,820
No.

445
00:45:51,910 --> 00:45:54,990
Well, what if he wasn't allowed to kick,
and you were really mad at him?

446
00:45:55,120 --> 00:45:57,080
No, Joseph.

447
00:46:38,450 --> 00:46:41,160
How much did you take off?

448
00:46:43,040 --> 00:46:45,590
I lied.

449
00:46:48,130 --> 00:46:50,090
You added?

450
00:46:58,010 --> 00:46:59,970
How much is it?

451
00:47:01,480 --> 00:47:03,440
270 pounds.

452
00:47:07,020 --> 00:47:08,980
Let's put more.

453
00:47:12,280 --> 00:47:13,740
Okay.

454
00:47:17,910 --> 00:47:20,660
Why don't you move back a little
just to be safe, okay?

455
00:47:53,530 --> 00:47:55,410
More?

456
00:48:06,710 --> 00:48:10,550
You should never do anything like this.
You know that, right?

457
00:48:12,340 --> 00:48:14,800
What do you do
if something bad happens?

458
00:48:14,920 --> 00:48:17,390
Get Mom.

459
00:48:17,510 --> 00:48:19,390
Right.

460
00:48:54,590 --> 00:48:57,380
How much did you put on that time?

461
00:49:01,300 --> 00:49:03,180
All of it.

462
00:49:04,930 --> 00:49:06,890
What else can we use?

463
00:49:33,540 --> 00:49:35,510
How much is it?

464
00:49:44,470 --> 00:49:46,430
How much is it, Dad?

465
00:49:49,440 --> 00:49:51,980
About 350 pounds.

466
00:49:59,200 --> 00:50:01,160
Fracture of the fifth metacarpal
of the right hand...

467
00:50:01,240 --> 00:50:04,580
as well as multiple fractures of
the sixth, seventh and eighth ribs.

468
00:50:04,700 --> 00:50:07,250
The worst of the injury, however,
was sustained to the right leg...

469
00:50:07,330 --> 00:50:09,290
in the form of a spiral fracture.

470
00:50:09,410 --> 00:50:11,370
There were 14 breaks.

471
00:50:11,500 --> 00:50:14,380
It simply shattered.

472
00:50:14,460 --> 00:50:17,000
They call me Mr. Glass.

473
00:50:17,130 --> 00:50:20,800
- Who does?
- Kids.

474
00:50:20,930 --> 00:50:22,970
Are you all right, Mr Price?

475
00:50:23,090 --> 00:50:25,050
Shall I continue?

476
00:50:26,430 --> 00:50:28,810
Pins were placed throughout
the length of the leg.

477
00:50:28,890 --> 00:50:31,640
The use of a wheelchair will be needed
for a two-month period.

478
00:50:31,730 --> 00:50:35,150
The use of crutches will follow
for 12 to 14 months.

479
00:50:35,230 --> 00:50:37,360
Hospital stay will range
from fiive to eight days...

480
00:50:37,480 --> 00:50:40,320
followed by 9 to 12 months
of physical therapy.

481
00:50:40,400 --> 00:50:43,110
Prescribed medication for pain
management will take the usual forms...

482
00:50:43,200 --> 00:50:45,990
of morphine drip, percocet, darvocet.

483
00:50:57,500 --> 00:50:59,550
Your, uh, 10:00 is here.

484
00:50:59,670 --> 00:51:01,800
The hospital discharged him
this morning.

485
00:51:04,010 --> 00:51:05,970
Thanks.

486
00:51:14,440 --> 00:51:15,900
Elijah, right?

487
00:51:15,980 --> 00:51:19,820
We're gonna prevent any substantial
atrophy ofyour good leg with this...

488
00:51:19,900 --> 00:51:22,110
and it works your quadriceps.

489
00:51:23,280 --> 00:51:25,490
How long have you been married?

490
00:51:28,200 --> 00:51:31,120
- Twelve years.
- How'd you get together?

491
00:51:33,410 --> 00:51:35,380
I'm a little nervous being here.

492
00:51:35,500 --> 00:51:38,920
I ask too many questions
when I'm nervous.

493
00:51:41,050 --> 00:51:43,010
A car accident.

494
00:51:43,130 --> 00:51:46,890
Oh. And now you're gonna
have to tell me more.

495
00:51:51,270 --> 00:51:53,730
My husband was a star athlete
in college...

496
00:51:53,850 --> 00:51:55,900
and we were in
an accident together.

497
00:51:57,520 --> 00:51:59,900
Our car flipped on an icy
road and we were both injured,

498
00:51:59,901 --> 00:52:01,570
and he couldn't play football any more.

499
00:52:01,650 --> 00:52:04,400
And if that hadn't happened,
we probably wouldn't have been together.

500
00:52:04,530 --> 00:52:08,200
- How so?
- I think we should talk about your rehab.

501
00:52:08,320 --> 00:52:10,620
You don't have to answer
if you don't want to.

502
00:52:11,790 --> 00:52:15,290
So, tell me more
about the quad machine.

503
00:52:16,620 --> 00:52:19,540
It prevents atrophy by--

504
00:52:21,300 --> 00:52:23,510
I couldn't spend my life
with someone who played football.

505
00:52:23,590 --> 00:52:25,550
It's pretty much that simple.

506
00:52:26,760 --> 00:52:29,930
I mean, I don't hate the game. I admire
the amount of skill it involves.

507
00:52:30,050 --> 00:52:32,810
And-And like everyone else,
I was in awe of the way he played it.

508
00:52:32,930 --> 00:52:37,150
But football, in many ways,
is the opposite of what I do.

509
00:52:38,440 --> 00:52:40,900
You're rewarded the more
you punish your opponent.

510
00:52:41,020 --> 00:52:44,440
You know, it's too much about violence,
and I don't want violence in my life.

511
00:52:45,780 --> 00:52:49,450
